<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quintus Lollius Urbicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 2nd century</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintus Lollius Urbicus was a Roman general from Numidia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> who served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from about 139 to 142 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a>. He reoccupied southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> between the Forth and the Clyde, moving the frontier north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. He later became prefect of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quintus-lollius-urbicus.jpg" alt="Quintus Lollius Urbicus" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIB_1276_Rochester.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was the son of Marcus Lollius Senecio, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> landowner, and his wife Grania Honorata, from Tiddis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> in Numidia, where a pair of inscriptions set out his career in detail. The historian Edward Champlin places him in a Cirtan community at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> that also included Publius Pactumeius Clemens and the orator Marcus Cornelius Fronto, for whom Urbicus and Pactumeius Clemens were later useful patrons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After a military tribunate with the Legio XXII Primigenia at Mogontiacum, he entered the Senate and served a year as legate to the proconsul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. As Hadrian's candidate he rose quickly, commanded the Legio X Gemina at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and received military decorations for service in Hadrian's Jewish War of 132–135. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> in 135 or 136, he then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> soon after Hadrian's death.</p><p>According to the Historia Augusta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> defeated the Britons through his legate Lollius Urbicus. Between 139 and 140 he refurbished the fort at Corbridge, and commemorative coins issued in 142 marked a victory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, so he probably led the reoccupation of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 141, including the capture of Burnswark Hill, with all three legions of the province. No source describes the campaign, but he likely advanced along Dere Street and campaigned against the Votadini, Selgovae, Damnonii and Novantae. He is named on building inscriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> from Balmuildy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and probably became praefectus urbi in 146, after the death of Sextus Erucius Clarus; he may have been the prefect known to have died in 160. As prefect he presided at the trial of a Christian, Ptolemaeus, who was sentenced to death, and the case led Justin Martyr to write his Apology. The historian Colin Wells saw his career, which took the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> landowner from a small inland town to the highest posts at Rome, as possible at no other period of history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quintus Lollius Urbicus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tiddis, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.46,6.48&amp;year=149" aria-label="Tiddis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Quintus Lollius Urbicus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5445 4436h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5445 4436h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.46,6.48&amp;year=149">What was here in 149 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
